---
import Film from '../../plate/Film.astro';
import { renderFormula } from '../../../lib/formulas/render';
import { CUES, TIMELINE } from './story';
import './style.css';

const labels = [
  { id: 'z3', tex: String.raw`\mathbb Z^3` },
  { id: 'r3', tex: String.raw`\mathbb R^3` },
  { id: 'tiling', tex: String.raw`A\oplus T=\mathbb Z^3` },
  { id: 'z2', tex: String.raw`\mathbb Z^2` },
  { id: 'r2', tex: String.raw`\mathbb R^2` },
  { id: 'lattice', tex: String.raw`A=\langle(-4,0),(-3,1)\rangle` },
  { id: 'thickening', tex: String.raw`\Omega=T+[0,1]^3` },
] as const;
---

<Film
  tag="uruk-same-tile"
  cues={CUES}
  timeline={TIMELINE}
  step={50}
  labels={labels}
  description="Copies of a domino fill finite grid windows by translations. Their left-cell markers become translation dots. Aligned rows give a fully periodic tiling; shifted rows fail only the tested vertical shifts within a twenty-four-row window. Exact square and L-tetromino examples are separate from schematic hexagons. Known integer-lattice dimensions lead to an explicitly schematic three-dimensional field. A dotted placeholder says the actual tile is not drawn. Blue marks the claim of no fully periodic tiling and does not compute a counterexample from those dots. A review ledger distinguishes the catalogued statement from approval."
  caption="One domino has a fully periodic aligned-row tiling. A second 24-row window fails vertical shifts 1–12. Such a finite check cannot determine an infinite extension's periodicity. The paper claims a three-dimensional tile with no fully periodic tiling. Its geometry is not drawn."
  leftOut="the Sudoku rule and cyclic-group construction; the Euclidean argument's grid-centres lemma; the hat tile; undecidability; Greenfeld and Tao's high-dimensional counterexample; the geometry of the paper's tile."
>
  <p class="film-method">How the film is made. The domino consists of the integer cells <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`(0,0),(1,0)`, false)} />. In row <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`r`, false)} />, its translation vectors are <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`(2j+s_r,r),\quad j\in\mathbb Z,\quad s_r\in\{0,1\}`, false)} />. The same tile covers each row exactly once for either shift. Tiles slide without rotation. Dots record the left-cell translation vectors, rather than the cells covered by the tile.</p>
  <p class="film-method">Aligned rows have shifts zero and periods <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`(2,0)`, false)} /> and <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`(0,1)`, false)} />, so they give a fully periodic tiling. The other window uses the shipped 24 shifts, 000111010110100010101010. Its horizontal period is still two. The finite comparison rejects vertical shifts from one to twelve wherever both rows are in the window. That excludes only those tested vertical matches, not every period or every infinite extension. Repeating the entire 24-row block would itself give a vertical period of 24. The window cannot prove that an infinite tiling is not fully periodic.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The L-tetromino uses cells <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`(0,0),(0,1),(0,2),(1,0)`, false)} /> and the translation lattice generated by <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`(-4,0),(-3,1)`, false)} />. Its fundamental area is four, matching the four cells, and the actual covering calculation checks the cells rather than relying only on that area. The square patch is exact. The hexagon example is schematic and is not a computed cover.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The conjecture asks whether a tile that admits any translational tiling admits at least one fully periodic tiling. The aligned domino example answers that question for this domino even though it has many other arrangements. The known two-dimensional result on the ladder concerns finite tiles of <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\mathbb Z^2`, false)} />. The periodic-tiling conjecture for arbitrary measurable tiles of <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\mathbb R^2`, false)} /> remains open. The film does not extend Bhattacharya's lattice theorem to that broader Euclidean class.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The paper's three-dimensional tile is not constructed or drawn here. Its dotted box is a labelled placeholder, not a cube-shaped tile. The three-dimensional translation dots, broken arrows and possible single arrow are schematic claim diagrams. They are not the paper's translation set, a computed counterexample or tests of periodicity on that tile. The arrows do not derive the claim from the displayed dots.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The claim is that a finite nonempty tile of <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\mathbb Z^3`, false)} /> tiles but admits no fully periodic tiling, and that its unit-cube thickening in <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\mathbb R^3`, false)} /> has the same property even when arbitrary real translation vectors are allowed. Full periodicity means invariance under a finite-index subgroup in the integer lattice, or a full-rank lattice in Euclidean space. A single period is not excluded by the stated theorem. The remaining dashed single arrow means only that such a period is not ruled out; it neither constructs nor proves the existence of a partially periodic tiling of the claimed tile.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The closing evidence follows the article's date, 18 October 2026. No mathematician had publicly reported reading or checking the proof. PeriodicTilingThree.lean is catalogued. Besides the two three-dimensional claims, the statement adds a least-dimension clause whose lower-dimensional theorems are used but not proved by the paper; the formal development therefore needs its own proofs for those dimensions. No outside replay was found. A kernel check is evidence about a formal statement, not approval or a reading of the paper. Without JavaScript, the article and this transcript remain available.</p>
</Film>

<script>
  import './element';
</script>
